-- Seed original introductory study summaries. Never overwrite a teacher-edited summary.
insert into public.nammath_notes (topic_id,summary,formulas,example,tip,pitfall,published) values ('m1-equations','Manipulate algebraic expressions before solving equations and inequalities. Distinguish an equation (true for particular values), an identity (true for all permitted values) and an inequality. Always check restrictions introduced by denominators or radicals.','(a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b²
(a − b)² = a² − 2ab + b²
ax² + bx + c = 0 ⇒ x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/(2a)','Solve x² − 5x + 6 = 0. Factorise: (x − 2)(x − 3) = 0, so x = 2 or x = 3.','Show each transformation and check both solutions in the original equation. For inequalities, reverse the sign when multiplying or dividing by a negative quantity.','Cancelling a factor without recording excluded values may lose or introduce solutions.',true) on conflict (topic_id) do nothing;
insert into public.nammath_notes (topic_id,summary,formulas,example,tip,pitfall,published) values ('m1-sequences','An arithmetic progression has a constant difference d, while a geometric progression has a constant ratio r. Identify the sequence type before selecting the term or sum formula. For an infinite geometric series, convergence requires |r| < 1.','AP: uₙ = a + (n − 1)d
AP: Sₙ = n[2a + (n − 1)d]/2
GP: uₙ = arⁿ⁻¹
GP: Sₙ = a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r), r ≠ 1
GP infinity: S∞ = a/(1 − r), |r| < 1','For 3, 6, 12, … we have a = 3 and r = 2. The fifth term is u₅ = 3(2⁴) = 48.','Write down a, d or r explicitly. For infinite sums, state the convergence condition before substituting.','Using the infinite geometric-series formula when |r| ≥ 1.',true) on conflict (topic_id) do nothing;
insert into public.nammath_notes (topic_id,summary,formulas,example,tip,pitfall,published) values ('m1-graphs','A function maps inputs in its domain to outputs. Read intercepts, symmetry, turning points, asymptotes and transformations from the rule or graph. Inverse functions reverse the mapping on a suitable restricted domain.','Translation: y = f(x − a) + b shifts by (a, b)
Reflection in x-axis: y = −f(x)
Inverse relation: interchange x and y, then solve for y','If f(x) = 2x − 3, let y = 2x − 3. Swap variables: x = 2y − 3, hence f⁻¹(x) = (x + 3)/2.','Label important coordinates, state domain restrictions, and check whether an inverse is a function.','Confusing horizontal changes inside f(·) with vertical changes outside it.',true) on conflict (topic_id) do nothing;
insert into public.nammath_notes (topic_id,summary,formulas,example,tip,pitfall,published) values ('m1-coordinate','Coordinate geometry links algebraic equations with line properties. Gradient measures steepness; parallel lines have equal gradients and perpendicular non-vertical lines have gradients whose product is −1.','m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁)
y − y₁ = m(x − x₁)
Midpoint = ((x₁ + x₂)/2, (y₁ + y₂)/2)
Distance = √[(x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²]','Through (1, 2) and (3, 6), m = (6 − 2)/(3 − 1) = 2, so y − 2 = 2(x − 1), or y = 2x.','Substitute a given point back into your line equation to verify the constant term.','Reversing the y-coordinate order without also reversing the x-coordinate order.',true) on conflict (topic_id) do nothing;
insert into public.nammath_notes (topic_id,summary,formulas,example,tip,pitfall,published) values ('m1-circular','Radian measure links an angle to the arc of a circle. Convert degrees to radians when required, and use radians for arc-length and sector-area formulas.','180° = π radians
Arc length s = rθ
Sector area A = ½r²θ','A sector has r = 6 cm and θ = π/3. Its arc length is 6π/3 = 2π cm, and area is ½(36)(π/3) = 6π cm².','Check your calculator angle mode; attach units to length and area answers.','Substituting degrees directly into s = rθ or A = ½r²θ.',true) on conflict (topic_id) do nothing;
insert into public.nammath_notes (topic_id,summary,formulas,example,tip,pitfall,published) values ('m1-trig','Trigonometric ratios connect angles and side lengths; unit-circle symmetry helps solve equations over a stated interval. Sketch or reason about the sign of sine, cosine and tangent in each quadrant.','sin²θ + cos²θ = 1
tan θ = sin θ / cos θ, cos θ ≠ 0
sin(−θ) = −sin θ; cos(−θ) = cos θ','Solve sin θ = ½ for 0° ≤ θ ≤ 360°. The reference angle is 30°, giving θ = 30° and θ = 150°.','State every solution in the specified interval and check if the interval endpoints are included.','Giving only the principal calculator angle and overlooking other quadrants.',true) on conflict (topic_id) do nothing;
insert into public.nammath_notes (topic_id,summary,formulas,example,tip,pitfall,published) values ('m1-vectors','A three-dimensional vector describes displacement in x, y and z directions. Work component by component to add vectors, calculate magnitude or find a dot product.','|a| = √(a₁² + a₂² + a₃²)
a · b = a₁b₁ + a₂b₂ + a₃b₃
a · b = |a||b|cos θ','For a = (2, −1, 2), |a| = √(4 + 1 + 4) = 3.','Use consistent vector notation and check dimensions before interpreting an angle or projection.','Treating vectors as ordinary numbers or dropping a negative component in a dot product.',true) on conflict (topic_id) do nothing;
insert into public.nammath_notes (topic_id,summary,formulas,example,tip,pitfall,published) values ('m1-diff','Differentiation gives the instantaneous rate of change or tangent gradient. Use derivatives to find stationary points, tangent equations and maxima or minima. In motion problems, velocity is the derivative of displacement.','d(xⁿ)/dx = nxⁿ⁻¹
Stationary point: f′(x) = 0
If f″(x) > 0: local minimum; if f″(x) < 0: local maximum
v = ds/dt, a = dv/dt','For f(x) = x² − 6x + 5, f′(x) = 2x − 6 = 0 at x = 3. f(3) = −4 and f″(x) = 2 > 0, so (3, −4) is a minimum.','For rates of change, write what each derivative represents and include units. For optimisation, classify your stationary point.','Confusing the coordinate of a turning point with its derivative, which is zero there.',true) on conflict (topic_id) do nothing;
insert into public.nammath_notes (topic_id,summary,formulas,example,tip,pitfall,published) values ('m1-integration','Integration reverses differentiation and can calculate accumulated change or signed area. Indefinite integrals need a constant C; definite integrals use upper and lower limits.','∫xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1) + C, n ≠ −1
∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx = F(b) − F(a)
Area between curve and x-axis may require splitting at roots','∫(6x² − 4x) dx = 2x³ − 2x² + C.','Differentiate an indefinite integral result to check it. For area, consider where the integrand is negative.','Forgetting +C, or treating a signed integral as always equal to geometric area.',true) on conflict (topic_id) do nothing;
insert into public.nammath_notes (topic_id,summary,formulas,example,tip,pitfall,published) values ('m2-algebra','Further algebra builds accuracy with rearranging, factorisation, equations and restrictions. Choose a method that preserves equivalence and identify values for which the original expression is undefined.','a² − b² = (a − b)(a + b)
(a/b) ÷ (c/d) = ad/(bc), provided all denominators are nonzero
For quadratics, discriminant Δ = b² − 4ac','(x² − 9)/(x − 3) = [(x − 3)(x + 3)]/(x − 3) = x + 3 for x ≠ 3.','Annotate excluded values before cancelling, and verify resulting values in the original problem.','Assuming simplification removes restrictions on the original expression.',true) on conflict (topic_id) do nothing;
insert into public.nammath_notes (topic_id,summary,formulas,example,tip,pitfall,published) values ('m2-logs','Exponential and logarithmic functions are inverses. Their laws simplify products, quotients and powers, but logarithm arguments must be strictly positive.','logₐ(xy) = logₐ x + logₐ y
logₐ(x/y) = logₐ x − logₐ y
logₐ(xⁿ) = n logₐ x
ln(eˣ) = x; e^(ln x) = x for x > 0','Solve ln x = 2: exponentiate both sides to get x = e².','State the domain condition before solving any logarithmic equation and check the final answer.','Using ln(x + y) = ln x + ln y, which is false.',true) on conflict (topic_id) do nothing;
insert into public.nammath_notes (topic_id,summary,formulas,example,tip,pitfall,published) values ('m2-trig','Further trigonometry extends identities and equation solving. Rearrange carefully, factor when possible and identify all angles satisfying both the identity and the interval.','sin 2x = 2sin x cos x
cos 2x = cos²x − sin²x = 1 − 2sin²x
1 + tan²x = sec²x (where defined)','Solve cos 2x = 0 for 0° ≤ x < 180°. Then 2x = 90° or 270°, so x = 45° or 135°.','When multiplying an angle by 2, double the interval as well, and transform the solutions back at the end.','Dividing an equation by sin x or cos x without separately checking when that factor equals zero.',true) on conflict (topic_id) do nothing;
insert into public.nammath_notes (topic_id,summary,formulas,example,tip,pitfall,published) values ('m2-diff','Further differentiation combines chain, product and quotient rules with logarithmic and exponential functions. It supports more sophisticated tangents, stationary points and related rates of change.','Chain rule: d[f(g(x))]/dx = f′(g(x))g′(x)
Product rule: (uv)′ = u′v + uv′
Quotient rule: (u/v)′ = (vu′ − uv′)/v²
d(eˣ)/dx = eˣ; d(ln x)/dx = 1/x (x > 0)','For y = (3x + 1)⁴, y′ = 4(3x + 1)³ · 3 = 12(3x + 1)³.','Write the inner function and its derivative before applying the chain rule.','Forgetting the derivative of the inner expression, or swapping terms in the quotient rule.',true) on conflict (topic_id) do nothing;
insert into public.nammath_notes (topic_id,summary,formulas,example,tip,pitfall,published) values ('m2-integration','Further integration includes standard exponential, logarithmic and trigonometric integrals, often after a substitution. Use definite integration for areas and, where specified, solids of revolution.','∫e^(ax) dx = e^(ax)/a + C (a ≠ 0)
∫(1/x) dx = ln|x| + C
∫cos(ax) dx = sin(ax)/a + C
Volume about x-axis: V = π∫ₐᵇ[y(x)]² dx','∫2cos(2x) dx = sin(2x) + C.','Label the axis of rotation and check the correct limits, units and squared radius for a volume problem.','Forgetting the factor 1/a when integrating a function of ax.',true) on conflict (topic_id) do nothing;
insert into public.nammath_notes (topic_id,summary,formulas,example,tip,pitfall,published) values ('m2-numerical','Numerical methods estimate roots when exact algebraic solutions are difficult. Iteration produces successive approximations; Newton–Raphson uses the tangent to improve an estimate. Confirm that estimates meet the required accuracy.','Fixed-point iteration: xₙ₊₁ = g(xₙ)
Newton–Raphson: xₙ₊₁ = xₙ − f(xₙ)/f′(xₙ)','To solve x² − 2 = 0 with x₀ = 1.5: x₁ = 1.5 − (1.5² − 2)/(2 × 1.5) ≈ 1.41667.','Show each iteration with sufficient working precision and round only at the final requested step.','Using a starting estimate where f′(x) = 0, or rounding intermediate values too early.',true) on conflict (topic_id) do nothing;
